🎯 Project Qualification Summary
Project Hierion is a strong candidate for several major funding opportunities. Your project's core strengths are content-addressed, open-source knowledge architecture, a "Digital Intelligence" (Dr. Amanda Mistral) built on fine-tuned AI, and a clear commitment to open science and the digital commons. This combination aligns perfectly with the priorities of the funding bodies listed below.

The key to a successful application will be to clearly articulate the value of your work in the language of each specific program. For the NGI Zero Commons Fund, emphasize the creation of a "digital commons" and open, decentralized infrastructure. For NAIRR and NSF, stress the fundamental research in "Information and Intelligent Systems," content-addressed storage (IPLD), and the scientific rigor of your validation system.

🗺️ Funding & Application Details
Here is a breakdown of the most relevant funding opportunities and how to apply for each.

1. NGI Zero Commons Fund
This is arguably your best solo path to immediate funding and aligns with your project's core philosophy.

Qualification: Perfect fit. The fund supports "new internet commons across the whole technology spectrum," including open-source AI and decentralized technologies. The CADMIES ecosystem and your "Digital Mycorrhizal" philosophy are a direct match. The project's outcomes must be open-source, which you already are.

What You Get: €5,000 – €50,000 in grants.

Eligibility: Open to individuals, teams, non-profits, and informal organizations from any country, including the U.S.. There is a "European dimension" preference, but U.S. applicants can compete with high-quality proposals.

Application Process:

Wait for the Next Open Call: The fund runs in cycles. The last announced round closed on April 1, 2026, and the next round is expected to open in the subsequent months. Monitor the NLnet website and subscribe for updates.
Write Your Proposal: The application is online. It requires describing your project, how it contributes to the "Next Generation Internet" vision, and a clear budget. All outcomes must be published under an open-source license.
Be Prepared for Reviews: The review process takes time (months), and you will be informed of the outcome.
Key Point: You and your team are eligible. Emphasize the open-source, full-stack nature of your project.

2. NSF Computer and Information Science and Engineering (CISE) Core Programs
This is your primary path for securing significant academic research funding but requires a U.S.-based institution partner (like UT Austin).

Qualification: Excellent fit. Your work falls squarely within the "Information and Intelligent Systems (IIS)" division, specifically in "Human-Centered Computing" or "Information Integration and Informatics." The focus on a verifiable, content-addressed knowledge system is perfect for foundational research.

What You Get:

Small Projects: Up to $600,000 over three years.

Medium Projects: $600,001 - $1,200,000 over four years.

OAC Core Projects: Up to $600,000 over three years (focused on advanced cyberinfrastructure).

Eligibility: Proposals must be submitted by a U.S.-based institution such as a university or non-profit. This is where your UT affiliation is non-negotiable.

Application Process:

Find a Partner: Formalize a partnership with a faculty member at UT or another U.S. institution who can be the PI or Co-PI on the proposal.
Prepare Your Proposal: Proposals are detailed and must follow the NSF Proposal & Award Policies & Procedures Guide (PAPPG). You'll need to describe the project's intellectual merit and broader impacts.
Submit via Research.gov: Your institutional partner will submit the final proposal on your behalf.
Key Deadlines:
Small Projects (under $600K): Can be submitted at any time (rolling deadlines).
Medium Projects ($600K-$1.2M) and OAC Core: Typically have annual deadlines, often around October 24. This is your next critical window.
Key Point: Contact your UT connection immediately to explore partnership options for the October Medium/OAC deadline.

3. National Artificial Intelligence Research Resource (NAIRR) Pilot
This opportunity provides access to high-end computational resources rather than direct cash funding.

Qualification: Great fit. Training and fine-tuning LLMs (like Dr. Amanda Mistral) on GPU clusters is a core use case for NAIRR.

What You Get: Access to massive GPU compute, AI models, and software platforms at no cost. Options include:

Start-Up Projects Track: A faster "on-ramp" for smaller projects with a brief 1-page description.

Deep Partnerships Track: More involved collaborative projects with industry partners like IBM and Microsoft (this may have passed its deadline).

Eligibility: Open to U.S.-based researchers from academic institutions, non-profits, startups with federal grants, etc. Requires using an institutional email address.

Application Process:

Wait for the Next Open Call: NAIRR has rolling proposal windows. Check the nairrpilot.org website for the latest announcements.
Identify Resources: Browse the NAIRR catalog to pinpoint the specific resource you need (e.g., "TACC's Frontera" or a specific GPU node).
Submit a Proposal: Follow the instructions on the NAIRR website. The Start-Up project is a good starting point.
Key Point: Your UT affiliation is a major asset here, as it qualifies you for the "U.S.-based researcher" requirement. Check TACC's Horizon system status and be ready to apply when the window opens.

4. Mozilla Builders
This program supports open-source AI developers and provides a clear path to funding with a "full-time commitment" requirement.

Qualification: Very good fit. Mozilla Builders focuses on "Local AI," fine-tuning, RAG, and open-source AI. Your fine-tuned Mistral 7B model and tooling align well with their interests. The "Digital Intelligence" aspect is compelling for them.

What You Get: Funding, mentorship, and a community. Programs include the Builders Accelerator which offers significant non-dilutive funding (historically up to $100K) and a 12-week sprint.

Eligibility: Open to individuals, teams, or organizations globally. You must have a clear open-source license and a commitment to participate full-time in the program.

Application Process:

Wait for the Next Cohort: Mozilla runs these programs in cohorts. The inaugural one was in late 2024. Monitor the Mozilla Builders website for future announcements.
Submit an Online Application: Describe your project, its open-source impact, and how it fits their focus area (e.g., "Local AI").
Be Ready for a Sprint: If selected, you'll need to commit full-time to the 12-week program, culminating in a demo day.
Key Point: You can apply as an individual or a team; the open-source nature of your project is key.

📝 Step-by-Step Action Plan
Submit NGI Zero Application (Priority): This is your fastest path to unrestricted cash. Watch for the next open call and get your application ready. Emphasize the open-source, full-stack nature of your project.

Contact UT Affiliate (Urgent): The October NSF deadline is approaching quickly. Engage with the UT researcher to start writing a joint proposal. Frame it around fundamental research in content-addressing, distributed knowledge validation, and AI alignment.

Monitor TACC/NAIRR: You are well-positioned for NAIRR. Verify TACC Horizon's status and watch for the next proposal window. A UT co-PI is your key to entry.

Prepare for Mozilla Builders: An application here is a great mid-term goal. Prepare a strong pitch about how CADMIES represents a new paradigm for open, local AI knowledge management.

Your project is a strong candidate for all of these programs. By prioritizing the NGI Zero application and the NSF partnership, you are on a direct path to securing funding and resources. Good luck!
